@{
    RootModule        = 'ZellijTerminal.psm1'
    ModuleVersion     = '0.7.18'
    GUID              = 'cbeb77a8-d47e-4f13-aa58-231e6cadee15'
    Author            = 'zt contributors'
    CompanyName       = ''
    Copyright         = '(c) 2026 zt contributors. MIT licence.'
    Description       = 'zt - register, start, stop and restart the workspaces you run inside one Zellij session on Windows. Definitions live in git; per-device paths and live state do not. Claude Code sessions register themselves via the hook; arbitrary PowerShell commands work the same way.'

    # 7.0, and the distinction matters.
    #
    # The SCRIPTS in scripts\ are deliberately 5.1-clean - no ternary, no ??, no
    # && - because the Claude Code hook invokes them under powershell.exe and
    # must keep doing so. Their headers say as much and it is enforced by test.
    #
    # This MODULE is a different contract. install.ps1 junctions it onto the
    # pwsh 7 module path, which Windows PowerShell does not scan, so on 5.1 `zt`
    # never autoloads no matter how compatible the body happens to be. Declaring
    # 5.1 here described the syntax and lied about the product: it let a 5.1 user
    # satisfy the manifest and still have nothing work, with no error naming the
    # cause. The floor is what the thing needs to actually run.
    PowerShellVersion = '7.0'

    # Core only. There is no Desktop story - see above.
    CompatiblePSEditions = @('Core')

    # Listed by name, no wildcards: that is what lets PowerShell autoload the
    # module the first time `zt` is typed.
    FunctionsToExport = @(
        'Invoke-ZellijTerminal',
        'Get-ZellijTerminal',
        'Select-ZellijTerminal',
        'Register-ZellijTerminal',
        'Unregister-ZellijTerminal',
        'Publish-ZellijTerminal',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalRoot',
        'Set-ZellijTerminalRoot',
        'Start-ZellijTerminal',
        'Stop-ZellijTerminal',
        'Restart-ZellijTerminal',
        'Remove-ZellijTerminalTab',
        'Sync-ZellijTerminal',
        'Set-ZellijTerminalWaiting',
        'Switch-ZellijTerminal',
        'Connect-ZellijTerminal',
        'Suspend-ZellijTerminal',
        'Resume-ZellijTerminal',
        'Edit-ZellijTerminalConfig',
        'Test-ZellijTerminalConfig',
        'Test-ZellijTerminalPad',
        'Debug-ZellijTerminalPad',
        'Install-ZellijTerminalPad',
        'Set-ZellijTerminalPadDevice',
        'Uninstall-ZellijTerminalPad',
        'Test-ZellijTerminal',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalDiagnostic',
        'Test-ZellijTerminalPaste',
        'Repair-ZellijTerminalPaste',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalHotkey',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalSession',
        'Remove-ZellijTerminalSession',
        'Add-ZellijTerminalDock',
        'Get-ZellijTerminalDock',
        'Install-ZellijTerminal',
        'Uninstall-ZellijTerminal',
        'Export-ZellijTerminal',
        'Import-ZellijTerminal',
        'Start-ZellijTerminalSetup',
        'Show-ZellijTerminalPadGuide',
        'Show-ZellijTerminalPaletteGuide'
    )
    CmdletsToExport   = @()
    VariablesToExport = @()
    AliasesToExport   = @('zt', 'zac')

    PrivateData       = @{
        PSData = @{
            Tags         = @('Zellij', 'ClaudeCode', 'MacroPad', 'Windows', 'Workspace', 'Productivity')
            LicenseUri   = 'https://github.com/ztpwsh/zellij-terminal/blob/main/LICENSE'
            ProjectUri   = 'https://github.com/ztpwsh/zellij-terminal'
            ReleaseNotes = '0.7.18 - CORRECTS 0.7.17, which explained this bug wrongly and shipped a fix that measures as a no-op. The symptom was right and the mechanism was not. A bar over its width budget is not wrapped and it is not truncated: the chunk that does not fit is DROPPED WHOLE, and which chunk loses is whichever one does not fit in the space the others left. So an over-long tab list does not shorten, it vanishes - every name at once - while the mode indicator and the activity codes carry on painting beside it. That all-or-nothing behaviour is why it reads as a rendering glitch rather than as the bar running out of room. This was established by rendering the bar and reading it back rather than by reasoning from the plugin source, which is what produced the wrong answer the first time. There was no way to see the bar: dump-screen on a plugin pane exits 0 and returns nothing, which looks like an empty bar rather than an unsupported operation. What works is that a headless zellij --layout client with redirected stdout writes its rendered screen as ANSI. Into a 280-column probe screen: 12 tabs painted every name; 14 tabs painted the mode, the padding and the codes, with not one tab name anywhere in the capture; and 8 tabs beside a 196-column strip painted every name and not one character of the strip. The fix is tab_display_count, which bounds what the tab list can ask for so it can never grow big enough to be dropped. zjstatus keeps a window of tabs around the ACTIVE one and reports the rest as a count, and clicks use the same window, so clicking still lands on the right tab. Set to 8, which sits above normal use rather than fitting a particular terminal. Note what that does and does not promise: it bounds the COUNT, not the WIDTH, so the real rule is still window times name length under your terminal width - at claude-<leaf> widths eight tabs wants about 190 columns. Verified at 4, 14 and 20 tabs against the settings lifted out of the deployed layout rather than a hand-typed copy of them: 4 shows everything with no marker, 14 shows eight and +6, 20 shows eight and +12, and the names survive in every case. format_hide_on_overlength, which 0.7.17 added, is REMOVED and its absence is now pinned by test. Rendered with and without it on the tabs-too-wide case the painted output was identical, because Zellij drops the over-wide chunk either way; and on 14 tabs it was worse, taking the activity codes away as well and still giving back no tab names. It is pinned because the name reads exactly like the fix, which is how it got in. zt check now reports the window rather than the guard, and warns if a deployed layout still carries the guard. What 0.7.17 got right and this keeps: the hook spends the strip''s columns on the projects that are waiting for you rather than naming every busy one, measured at 39 columns before and 17 after, which matters more now that the strip is the chunk most likely to be dropped. 0.7.17 - the tab names were scrolling off the status bar, which is the whole navigation surface: nothing to read and nothing to click, leaving only the pad''s blind next and prev. zjstatus composes the bar as format_left plus padding plus format_right, and the padding is cols.saturating_sub(left + right) - so when the two sides together are wider than the terminal there is no padding left to give and it does not shrink either side. It emits a line LONGER than the pane, the pane is one row, and the overflow wraps onto a row that does not exist, carrying the mode indicator and every tab name away with it. What survives on screen is the tail, which is the activity codes - so the bar looks like it is deliberately showing you the codes instead of the tabs. It arrives gradually rather than all at once, because it depends on the window width and on how many projects are busy, which makes it read as a rendering glitch rather than as configuration. Both sides grow together, once per project: a claude-<leaf> tab plus a glyph on the left, and a symbol-plus-name segment on the right while that project is busy. Three registered projects already wants about 136 columns, so a window that is merely not maximised is enough to lose the tabs. Two fixes, because one of them is a guard and the other is not overspending in the first place. The layout now sets format_hide_on_overlength true, which makes zjstatus drop a whole part rather than overflow; with format_precedence left at its default the part dropped is the RIGHT one, which is the correct sacrifice, since the activity codes are a convenience and the tab names are how you steer. And the hook now spends the strip''s columns on the projects that actually want you: a waiting project is named, because it may be on a tab you cannot see and you have to go and find it, while a project that is merely working contributes one coloured glyph and no name. Nothing is lost by that - its own tab carries the same glyph a few columns to the left, so printing the name here was printing it twice, and printing it twice is what pushed the tabs off. Measured on real hook output, three projects with one waiting: 39 columns before, 17 after. The wait flag already sat in the activity table beside the symbol and the colour and is now written into the state file, so what the strip promotes cannot drift from what the glyph says; a record written before this has no wait field, and $null -eq $true is false, so it reads as working and corrects itself on that project''s next event. zt check gains Tab names protected, because the deployed layout is a copy and can be older than the template it came from - a drift that does not look like a stale deployment, it looks like the tab bar breaking. Note that the layout is only read when a session STARTS, so an existing session must be deleted rather than killed: a killed one resurrects with the old bar. Also re-verified the rig against Zellij 0.45.0, by observation rather than by reading release notes - setup --check paths, a session building from default_layout with no client attached, the zjstatus pane present in what Zellij actually builds, and rename-tab -t targeting a tab without moving focus, which is what the activity glyph rests on. What was not re-tested is named as such rather than quietly relabelled. 0.7.16 - the live probe added in 0.7.14 was failing working machines, and this fixes it. Reported from a second PC: three failures in one run - the session reported no tabs, it carried no status bar pane, and the probe session was not removed - on a rig whose zt check said No failures and whose status bar was rendering perfectly. Nothing was wrong with it except that it was slower than three milliseconds. The probe waited for the session to be LISTED, which only means the Zellij server is up, and then judged all three claims from a single dump-layout taken immediately afterwards; the client is what hands the layout over, so the tabs and the plugin pane arrive after the name does. Measured on the machine this was written on: listed at 233 ms, built at 236 ms. That is not a margin, it is a coin toss with a bias, and it is exactly why it passed everywhere it was developed. The cleanup check had the mirror image of the same fault - one list-sessions at zero, when the server takes about 105 ms to stop listing a session it has been told to delete, so a clean removal was reported as a leaked session and the message told you to delete a session that had already deleted itself. Each claim is now polled until it is true or a fifteen second budget runs out, the elapsed time is reported either way so a slow machine is visible rather than mysterious, and a failure quotes what dump-layout actually returned - a Zellij error arrives in that same string, and no tabs is a poor description of session not found. The probe directory is also kept when anything failed, instead of being deleted unconditionally along with the Zellij stderr that was the only thing able to explain it. An installer that fails a working machine is worse than one that checks nothing, because the person then goes looking for a fault that is not there. 0.7.15 - the installer now knows when it is running inside the thing it is installing, and stops calling a deferred step a failed install. Both halves come from one question: what happens when somebody upgrades a machine that is already installed and already in use? They paste the one-liner into the pane they are working in, which is the obvious thing to do and the one case nothing here had ever considered - no part of this rig read ZELLIJ at all. Nesting works, so this reports rather than blocks, but it now names the three things that go wrong only there: a Zellij server is running by definition, so the zjstatus permission grant refuses; the module junction is swapped underneath a shell that has already imported it; and the printed instruction to delete every session includes the one it is being read in. That instruction is now the whole sequence rather than only its destructive half - zt park, delete-session for every name, install.ps1, zac, zt restore - because close every session and re-run is true and unusable: acting on it means discarding every Claude Code conversation that is open, so it gets ignored and the machine stays broken. park and restore already existed for exactly this and were never mentioned at the one moment they mattered. And a grant that could not be written now exits 2 rather than 1. Every machine upgrading from 0.7.9 or earlier while in use meets that case, and reporting an otherwise complete install as FAILED invites the single response that cannot help - run it again, with the server still up. Two is still non-zero, so nothing in a chain can treat it as clean; bootstrap.ps1 and the guided setup both branch on it, and the verification pass reports the grant as DEFER rather than BAD. 0.7.14 - the installer now proves its config produces a working session, instead of proving it wrote some files. Every check added so far reads a file, and that is not the same as the rig working: the evening this rig lost was spent on a machine where the layout, the config, the plugin binary and the permission grant were all provably correct and there was no status bar, because the session being attached to had been built before any of it. Nothing that reads files can see that. So install.ps1 ends by starting a session of its own from the config it just wrote, asking that session what it is made of, and deleting it. Zellij is client/server and `zellij action dump-layout` answers over the socket with NO CLIENT ATTACHED - the same property that lets the macro pad reach an unfocused session - so no window opens and no focus is stolen. It proves three things nothing else could: that default_layout is actually loaded rather than merely present, that the layout PARSES (a KDL error is reported by Zellij as "Session not found", which reads as a missing session and sends you hunting elsewhere), and that a new session really carries the status bar pane pointing at the wasm the layout names. The session is NAMED rather than left for Zellij to name, so cleanup is exact instead of inferred from a before-and-after list, and it is deleted rather than killed, because a killed session stays resurrectable. -SkipLiveProbe opts out. Two findings came out of building it, both verified rather than reasoned about. A layout naming a wasm that is not on disk produces a session with NO plugin pane at all, so that assertion is made only when the plugin is actually there - this installer deliberately does not download it. And ZELLIJ IGNORES %APPDATA%: it resolves the roaming folder through the Windows known-folder API, so redirecting that variable moves where this installer writes and not where Zellij reads. On an ordinary machine they are the same directory; where something redirects it, every file lands where Zellij never looks and every file check still passes, because each re-reads its own output at its own path. The installer now asks `zellij setup --check` where it reads and fails if that disagrees with where it wrote. Also proved by trying and failing: dumping the rendered bar to diff against a known-good capture. dump-screen takes --pane-id and accepts plugin ids, so it looks like the answer and is not - a plugin pane returns zero bytes, tested across every id from 0 to 8 with a client attached, because a plugin pane is not a terminal grid. The bar pixels stay unverifiable by machine; its presence does not. And `zellij pipe`, documented in the hook as never returning while zjstatus listens, returned in 135 ms on a machine with a working bar, so it does not discriminate either. tests/LiveSession.Tests.ps1 exercises the cycle against the real deployed config - it cannot be sandboxed, for the APPDATA reason above - and skips where Zellij is absent. 0.7.13 - 0.7.12 went red on CI within minutes, on its own new verification step, and the reason is the best argument for that step existing. It asked whether the module resolved by NAME, through Get-Module -ListAvailable, which searches $env:PSModulePath. On every development machine that answered yes - because a ZellijTerminal was already on that path from an earlier install - and on a clean runner installing to a directory not on that path it correctly answered no. A check that passes for a reason unrelated to what the installer just did is exactly the failure the verification step was added to catch, and it was caught in its first hour by the only kind of machine that could catch it: one with none of the state. The verification now asks about the destination THIS RUN used. It does not recompute that path either, because Resolve-ZtUserModulePath deliberately does not guess - it takes the user-scope entry already on PSModulePath, since Documents is redirected under OneDrive on some profiles including one of this project own, and a guessed path installs where PowerShell never looks. Recomputing would have made a fourth copy of a rule this repo already pins three copies of. So the installer asks the module, inside the module own scope, and a machine whose Documents are redirected gets the redirected answer. 0.7.12 - the installer now reads back its own work, and a test fails when a fresh install would. Everything before this release fixed a specific bug; this one is about the class of bug, because a rig whose install path is only ever exercised on the machine that wrote it will break the same way again with a different cause. Two things were missing. install.ps1 never re-read a single file it wrote, so "did not throw" was doing the work of "worked" - and those differ exactly where it matters: a permission grant written under a live Zellij server is deleted by that server minutes later, and a layout written with an unsubstituted {{PLUGINS}} is a file that exists, parses, and silently has no status bar. It printed Done nine times on a machine with no bar. There is now a [4/4] Verifying step that re-reads everything it claims: the module resolves by name, config.kdl and claude.kdl exist, the layout has no {{MARKER}} left in it, the plugin permission grant names the same path the layout does - the two are computed in different places and could drift - and the hook registration points at a script that is actually there. Any BAD becomes a recorded problem, so the closing banner cannot say Done and the exit code is non-zero, which bootstrap.ps1 reads. The second gap was that nothing exercised a cold install at all. tests/FreshInstall.Tests.ps1 runs the real installer against redirected APPDATA and LOCALAPPDATA - a profile with no Zellij config, no layout, no plugin, no grant and no module, which is every machine except a developer own - and asserts the verification pass reports no BAD, that the layout is fully substituted, that config.kdl loads it, and that the grant exists keyed by the path the layout names. On CI, which has no Zellij, that is as close to a fresh Windows box as a test can get. It was proved by breaking it: with the grant write commented out, the installer still printed "(zjstatus granted)", its own verify pass caught the lie, and five of the seven assertions went red. That is the property worth having - not that the installer is careful, but that carelessness is detected somewhere other than on somebody second PC a week later. Deliberately not asserted: anything needing a live session, a client or a rendered bar, all of which are legitimately false one second after a correct install. 0.7.11 - 0.7.10 granted the permission and the status bar still did not appear, for a second reason that hid behind the first. session_serialization true is set deliberately in config.kdl, so a session that exits stays listed and attach --create RESURRECTS it from its serialized state rather than building a new one from the layout. Everything changed since is therefore never read: a rewritten layout, an edited config, a plugin permission granted. Reinstalling changes nothing, however many times you do it, and the machine this was found on was reinstalled nine times in one evening. A killed session resurrects too - only delete-session clears it - and nothing in this rig said so anywhere. The proof is in the file: the installer writes the three permissions in a fixed order and the file came back in a different one, so the copy on disk was written by the Zellij server rather than by us, which is also why writing a grant underneath a live session is undone minutes later. install.ps1 therefore checks for a running server BEFORE writing rather than warning after. As a warning it printed while the file was already on disk and already doomed, and it read as advice about next time rather than as a statement that the run had just failed; it now refuses, records a problem so the closing banner cannot say Done, and names the way out. -SkipServerCheck overrides it, because a process check is only a proxy for the real question - whether that server shares this profile - and it does not when %LOCALAPPDATA% is redirected, which is how the suite runs the installer. zt check gains a Resurrectable sessions row and zt diag raises a signal for the same thing, both naming delete-session rather than restart. And a correction: 0.7.10 said the permission prompt could not be drawn in a one-row pane. That was wrong, and it was wrong in eight files. A screenshot from the machine being diagnosed shows it rendering perfectly well, as a single line reading "This plugin asks permission to: ... Allow? (y/n)". What it never receives is the KEYPRESS - the session starts in locked mode with focus in the terminal pane below, so y goes there instead and the prompt sits unanswered looking like a banner rather than a question. The distinction matters to anyone trying to answer it by hand, and the lesson is worth the lines it cost: an explanation invented to fit a symptom fits it exactly as well as the true one until somebody photographs the screen. Three assertions added - that the server check precedes the write, that the refusal happens and names delete-session where there is a server to refuse for, and that both the installer and the layer check say delete-session rather than kill. 0.7.10 - the status bar never worked on a machine that had not been used to develop it, and nothing anywhere said so. Zellij gates plugins behind a permission grant. Ungranted, zjstatus loads and is held pending approval, and the approval prompt renders IN THE PLUGIN OWN PANE - which the layout declares as one borderless row, in a session that starts locked. A dialog cannot draw in one row and no keystroke reaches that pane to accept it, so the outcome is no bar, no prompt, no error, and nothing in any log. The grant is acquired interactively, once, and cached in %LOCALAPPDATA%\Zellij\cache\permissions.kdl - outside the clone, outside %APPDATA%, and named nowhere in this repository until now. So every development machine had one and nothing that ships did, and no test could see the difference: it is state the published set could not carry, in a directory nothing looked at. Found on a second PC where the deployed layout was correct, the wasm was byte-identical to a working machine, the config set default_layout, the hook was demonstrably firing, and zt check reported No failures on every single line. install.ps1 now writes the grant beside the layout that references the plugin, because the permission that makes it load belongs in the same step as the file that names it. It MERGES rather than replacing: that file is Zellij own and can hold grants a person approved by hand, and silently revoking one reproduces this same invisible failure somewhere else. It is idempotent, keyed by the plugin absolute path with forward slashes - Zellij own form, taken from a file it wrote itself, so the grant is per machine and copying it between them grants nothing - and it is written even when the wasm is not there yet, since the grant is a statement about a path and downloading the plugin afterwards then works without a second install. That is the order the machine which hit this actually did it in. It also warns when a zellij server is running, because the server owns that file and writes its own copy back when it exits, so a grant written underneath a live session can be replaced by the state that session started with. zt check gains a zjstatus permitted row, which is the line that would have caught this on day one, and zt diag reads the cache directory it previously stopped one directory short of - the bundle listed %APPDATA%\Zellij and the answer was under %LOCALAPPDATA%. Nine assertions, six of them behavioural: they run the installer into a redirected profile and then run the layer check against the same redirected profile, so what is pinned is that the reader looks where the writer wrote rather than that three files contain matching text. One of them deletes the grant and asserts the new row reports FAIL, because a check that cannot fire is worse than no check and this entire entry exists because a table of passes described a machine with no status bar. 0.7.9 - zt diag, for the failure zt check cannot see. The layer check reports PASS on a machine where the rig does not work, and it is right to: every check in it asks whether a file is THERE, and the two files that decide whether a session starts are generated per machine and exist in no repository. %APPDATA%\Zellij\config\layouts\claude.kdl carries an absolute plugin path and an absolute cwd, both substituted at install time from a template. A layout that exists, parses, and points its plugin at a zjstatus.wasm nobody downloaded passes every question the layer check knows how to ask, and gives you a session with no status bar. An unreplaced {{PLUGINS}} does the same and takes the tab working directory with it. Neither prints anything, and install.ps1 says so in passing - it notes that the wasm is missing and carries on, so a clean install has no status bar by design. So zt diag collects rather than judges. It reads those files out verbatim and, where there is a source to compare against, regenerates what the installer should have written and diffs it - which on a machine with two clones names the clone that installed, in one line. Twelve sections: every ZellijTerminal on the module path and what each junction really resolves to, because a fix verified against the wrong tree looks exactly like a fix that works; zellij setup --check rather than our own Join-Path, since this project has already shipped one path missing the config level and a check that passed while reading nothing; the deployed layout and config; the plugin with a sha256, because the binary carries no version string worth trusting and a wasm built for a different plugin API loads, does nothing, and reports nothing; the hook registration with every stamped-in path tested; the registry; Windows Terminal; the environment; and finally zt check itself, so a clean table and a signal can be read side by side. The bundle opens with SIGNALS - observations, never verdicts, deduplicated - and is redacted by default, because it is written to be sent to somebody. It changes nothing on the machine. -ParseLayout is the single exception and says what it does: it is the only way to tell a KDL error from a missing session, because Zellij reports the first as the second, and --layout cannot be combined with --session, so the throwaway session it creates cannot be named in advance. The session list is captured before and after and only what appeared gets deleted. Two things the first run taught, both kept as comments where they happened. The layout template ships forty lines of commented worked examples, so scanning the raw text reported C:/code/api, api and web as tab directories that do not exist - four signals, every one of them prose. Every pattern now reads a comment-stripped copy, with the quote state tracked so a file:// location survives the stripping. And the escape-sequence cleaner uses [char]27 rather than the PowerShell 6 backtick-e escape: 5.1 does not error on an unknown backtick escape, it yields the literal character, so the pattern would have matched a letter e and quietly deleted real text out of the evidence. It parses either way, which is exactly why the 5.1 compat gate could not have caught it. 0.7.8 - zt setup could not complete a single one of its offers. Accept either of the two that call install.ps1 - write the Zellij config and layout, or register the hook for every project - and the installer got as far as its module step, printed the installed banner, and then died on "The term Write-Step is not recognized", a function it defines at the top of itself and had used four lines earlier. Nothing was wrong with install.ps1. A script invoked with the call operator from a MODULE function runs inside that module session state, and install.ps1 removes and re-imports ZellijTerminal as its first real step - which throws away the scope its own helpers were defined into, so every function it had not called yet stopped existing halfway through the run. Running install.ps1 from a shell was never affected, which is why the suite and every hand-run install stayed green: the guided path was the only caller with a module underneath it. It now starts a child pwsh. That is also the honest lifetime, because the installer replaces the module the guide is executing from and the copy already loaded stays loaded regardless - which is why install.ps1 has always ended by telling you to open a new shell. The child exit code is read, so an install that failed - a junction already pointing at a different clone, say - stops the guide walking on to the next step as though it had worked. The regression test rebuilds the mechanism from a module and a script of its own with no zt involved, because a fix is only worth keeping if the reason for it can still be demonstrated by somebody who was not there, and it pins the call shape both ways: the guard matches the old line and not the new one. On a clone that predates this, install.ps1 -Global run by hand from the clone does the same work and was never broken. 0.7.7 - Ctrl+V inside a Zellij pane pasted one line at a time, and in Claude Code every newline submits, so a ten-line paste became ten prompts. Windows Terminal wraps a paste in the bracketed-paste sequence only when the application it is talking to has enabled mode 2004, and inside a session that application is Zellij rather than the pane. Zellij 0.44.3 on Windows never asks, so Terminal types the clipboard in as ordinary keystrokes; Zellij never sees a paste either, so it cannot bracket the inner hop to the pane. One missing negotiation, both hops broken. It reads as a Claude Code bug and then as a TERM or NO_COLOR bug and is neither, and the way to prove the layer is to paste two lines at a plain pwsh prompt inside a pane with nothing else running. zt paste reports both layers and zt paste fix repairs them: Terminal stops owning ctrl+v so the keystroke reaches the application, and Claude Code gains a ctrl+v of its own pointed at chat:imagePaste, which despite the name is the clipboard read and handles text. Either half alone is worse than the bug, because Terminal unbound with no Claude binding leaves nothing pasting there at all. Deliberately not part of install.ps1: the installer reaches Terminal through fragments precisely so it never rewrites settings.json, which is JSONC and loses every comment to a ConvertTo-Json round trip. So the rewrite is a targeted text edit, it happens only when asked for by name, and it takes a backup first. alt+v always worked and still does. zt check gained two rows. Paste (Ctrl+V) reports the above, and a missing ctrl+v entry counts as broken: Terminal binds it by default, so reading silence as neutrality would report a clean bill of health on every unfixed machine. Release worktree FAILS when a registered workspace or a live session record sits inside a generated git worktree - Publish-Release.ps1 empties that directory on every run and rebuilds it from the manifest, so a session living there loses its files to the next publish and holds the directory open so the publish fails. Both have happened. It reads git worktree list rather than hard-coding a path, because that path is not a constant. Finally, a dead Claude session no longer reports as running. SessionEnd deletes the live record, the waiting flag, the status entry and the tab cache, but Claude Code cancels hooks that have not finished when it exits - Hook cancelled, for every hook registered, not just this one - and powershell.exe alone takes ~500 ms to start, so losing that race is normal rather than exceptional. The tab outlives the session too, because the pane drops back to a shell, and both survivors present read as running indefinitely, with zt go jumping to a dead session. The hook now records CLAUDE_PID, which Claude Code sets in the environment of everything it spawns and which costs nothing to read, so a reader can prove the session is gone; startedAt guards against a reused pid. The record is still KEPT when the process is dead, because leftover records are the entire input to zt restore, which reopens what a crash took down - deleting them would have turned shutdown recovery into a silent no-op. It reads as stale instead, and zt sync remains the only thing that removes it. The rule is written twice, in Core.ps1 and in the palette ZtStore.cs, because the palette reads these files directly and must not answer differently from zt. 0.7.6 - anyone who cloned before 0.7.5 could not update, and the failure named nothing that would explain itself. 0.7.5 rebuilt the published branch as a fresh orphan and force-pushed it, which is how a leaked blob is made unrecoverable and is expected to happen again. The cost lands on clones that already exist: their history is genuinely unrelated to the new one, so it can never fast-forward, and bootstrap.ps1 ran a bare git pull --ff-only. The one-liner therefore ended in "fatal: Not possible to fast-forward, aborting" and a failed install, permanently, for every returning user - reported from a machine still sitting on the pre-rebuild commit. It now fetches, tries the fast-forward, and on failure resets hard to the remote branch, saying that the history was rebuilt. It checks the tree is clean first and refuses while naming the files, because config/workspaces.json is tracked and is a file people reasonably edit in place. Deliberately no git clean: a clone from before 0.6 can still hold config/devices/<HOST>.json, a real device registry, untracked now only because that file moved to LOCALAPPDATA - and this project has lost that file to a clean once already. Four tests, one of which builds two real repositories and replaces the history of one, proving the fast-forward fails and the reset recovers rather than asserting it. If you are stuck on the old error, the fix cannot reach you through the thing that is broken: run git -C <clone> reset --hard origin/main once by hand, then the one-liner works again. 0.7.5 - the release an audit of the published repo paid for. The published history no longer carries a private lab note. A spike file shipped in 0.5.0 and deleted in 0.6.0 stayed recoverable from every clone, byte-identical to the private copy, while a shipped comment asserted that no spike is published and none ever will be. The branch was rebuilt as a fresh orphan and force-pushed - the tree is unchanged, so nothing anyone downloads differs - and the anonymisation gate now scans HISTORY as well as the tip: every path ever committed must still be on the manifest, and the personal-identifier needles run over every historical blob. It was run against the old history first, where it reports exactly the file that leaked. Related: zt hotkeys wrote its sample INTO a spikes directory inside the clone, creating the one directory this project promises is never published, with the device name in it; it goes to LOCALAPPDATA now. Seven places reported success that nothing had checked. Get-ZtTabNames returned the "Session not found" prose AS TAB NAMES, because query-tab-names prints it and exits 0. Test-ZtSession matched the session name as a substring of the whole session list, so with sessions claude and auspicious-pigeon live it answered yes to "auspicious", and would to "laud". zt stop announced "tab is now a shell" having observed nothing; it says what it SENT now. The pad path guard called ShortPath and never re-tested the result, which on a volume without 8.3 names returns the long path unchanged, so the remap was written and silently did nothing. The pad jump script wrote its state file after a go-to-tab-name that is a silent no-op when nothing is attached. bootstrap.ps1 never read the exit code install.ps1 sets for it, though install.ps1 names that omission in its own comment. And zj-claude-project.ps1 called zellij unguarded, so zt start without zellij threw the raw exception 0.7.4 claimed to have removed. Also: zt validate reported -Kind pwsh with no command as a fault, which is a documented, supported configuration. scripts/Test-Setup.ps1 promised Windows PowerShell 5.1 compatibility and used Start-Process -Environment, which arrived in 7.4 - so the one check written to prove the hook runs under 5.1 was guaranteed to fail there and only there. The hook corrected the tab name for a session running in a SUBFOLDER and then keyed its live record by the raw path anyway, so the workspace reported stopped while its session was plainly running and an orphan record was offered as a workspace of its own. The Command Palette extension passed -NonInteractive on every invocation including the visible ones, and under that flag Read-Host does not prompt - it fails and execution continues. So the five commands that end in "press enter to close" closed instantly, and "Define a root", which is two prompts, could never collect anything while still logging success. Its zellij helper redirected stderr and never read it, which can deadlock, with the timeout applied on the line after the one that blocks; kill and delete session reported success without checking; four user-visible actions wrote nothing to the log the README calls the first place to look; and zellij.exe was sought in exactly one directory before falling back to a PATH the module refuses to trust. pack.ps1 discarded the output of publish, makeappx and signtool, so a stranger whose build failed got a number and nothing else. The Windows SDK version, a hard build requirement, was documented only in a comment saying "only 10.0.26100.0 is installed here"; cmdpal/README.md now lists the prerequisites with their winget ids. Finally, the two surfaces share a vocabulary. The palette says "Unregister" and the CLI took only rm and remove - and remove routed to the unregister cmdlet while the cmdlet actually named Remove-ZellijTerminalTab was reached by close, so the one verb containing "remove" pointed at the opposite thing from the cmdlet named for it. Unregister-ZellijTerminal now answers to rm, remove, unregister and forget; Register-ZellijTerminal to add and register; tab completion knows them; and the documentation says in each place that the directory is never touched. 0.7.4 - zellij being absent is now a result rather than an exception. Every zt command that asked zellij anything went through one function that ran the executable without checking it was there, so on a machine where zellij is not installed - or simply not on PATH yet, which is the state install.ps1 exists to fix - the answer was a raw CommandNotFoundException from somewhere deep in the module instead of anything a person could act on. Get-ZtClientCount now reports -1, Test-ZtSession reports false, and zt sessions returns nothing, which is what all three of those already meant by "could not answer". Found by CI rather than by anyone using it: a hosted runner has no zellij, and the suite had been green on every machine that had the rig installed - which is exactly the population structurally unable to notice. The regression test empties PATH rather than filtering it, so it does not depend on where zellij happens to live. 0.7.3 - zac was blind to two thirds of Windows Terminal, and the check meant to catch that could never have fired. The window check matched process name WindowsTerminal only; Preview is WindowsTerminalPreview and Canary is WindowsTerminalCanary, so on either channel zac read the machine as having no window open and opened a SECOND client on a session that already had one - mirrored, with the grid pinned to the smaller, every time, with no self-correction. It then compared the Terminal window count before and after wt focus-tab to decide whether the focus was real. Terminal hosts every window in ONE PROCESS: opening a second window leaves that count at 1 and simply moves the MainWindowHandle of the process. The count could never rise, so the comparison reported success unconditionally and the branch handling the failure was unreachable - a check that cannot fail, which is worse than no check because it reads like evidence. Demonstrated rather than reasoned about, as was the reason the old comment was wrong: wt -w <unknown> focus-tab exits 0 and does NOTHING, watched for three seconds. focus-tab does not conjure windows; new-tab does. That check is gone. In its place is a question that can be answered: a zellij client is a PROCESS, so walking up from the tab running zellij attach reaches the terminal hosting it. zac now asks whether a Windows Terminal is showing THIS session, where before it asked whether any Terminal window existed anywhere on the machine - which was true while the client was attached from a different terminal application entirely. Nothing claims to have verified a focus any more, because there is no observable for one. A second bug, found by writing the test rather than by reading the code: zellij prints Session x not found and STILL EXITS 0, and the client parse counted every line that was not the header - so three lines of prose read as three attached clients and a session that does not exist reported as attached. Rows are counted by shape now, and the count returns -1 when zellij could not answer, so no such session and nobody watching can no longer be confused for each other. The cold path stops recording its window on the wt exit code, which is 0 the moment the tab is handed off and says nothing about whether zellij attach ever ran; the marker is written only once a client has actually arrived. The window name defaults to the session name, so attaching a second session from the palette no longer raises the window belonging to the first. tests/Attach.Tests.ps1 adds 27 assertions over the client parse, the Terminal firstWindowPreference read, and the decisions zac has to keep making. Documentation that could not be followed as written: zellij/README.md gave Zellij paths missing the config level and named a claude.kdl that is not in the repo - the layout ships as a template because it carries absolute paths - and docs/05-usage.md told you to copy that same missing file, so the by-hand install path was broken for anyone not running install.ps1. The user guide advertised zt hotkeys -Install, which is exported nowhere and reachable from no dispatch route; printed a sessions example with a literal list where a session name belongs; showed a workspace table three columns out of date that listed one id twice at one path, which the registry cannot hold; and began at <title> with no doctype and no charset while carrying 48 non-ASCII characters. Four published files justified the no-Ctrl+Alt rule by citing a development notes file that is never published, so the pointer was unfollowable by construction; they now cite D21 in docs/02-decisions.md, which states the same rule in public. The reference check that missed them was wrong three ways - the pattern could not see a bare filename, the question it asked was does this exist here rather than will the reader have it, and an extension allow-list hid the .ahk and .cs sites - and after fixing it was run against the previous commit to prove it reports all four. docs/00-background.md now records the mirroring behaviour itself rather than leaving it in a troubleshooting entry, including the consequence that appeared in no document at all: each client carries its OWN focus, so an injected keystroke can land in a pane you are not looking at. 0.7.2 - the tab says what its session is doing. A Claude tab is now named claude-web-api ~ while it reads, # while it writes, v when it has finished and wants you - the same table that colours the status bar, which could only ever say WHICH project was busy and never point at a tab. This was supposed to be impossible: rename-tab renames the FOCUSED tab, so a background hook calling it would rename whatever you were looking at. It turns out to take -t <id>, which targets a tab and leaves focus alone; docs/00-background.md had recorded the opposite as confirmed, and now records the retest. Colour cannot come along - zjstatus substitutes a tab name as plain text, so markup in it prints literally. The consequence to know about if you script against this rig: a tab s live name is no longer its identity. Strip the glyph before comparing and resolve back to the live name before go-to-tab-name, which matches exactly and no-ops silently on a miss. A session working in a SUBFOLDER of its project is now labelled by the project rather than by the folder: it used to invent a tab name that existed nowhere, which meant the one session actually asking for attention was the one key 3 refused to jump to. zac stops opening a second window on a session something is already viewing - Zellij mirrors, so that is one session with two clients, the same pane in both, your keystrokes in both, and the grid pinned to the SMALLEST client, which presents as a window that will not resize and text that will not reflow. The Windows Terminal tab is titled zellij-terminal and carries Zellij s icon, via a profile contributed as a Terminal FRAGMENT so your own settings.json is never edited; the icon is extracted from your installed zellij.exe rather than shipped here, and both are removed on uninstall. Terminal reads fragments at startup, so restart it once. zt check gained three lines: more than one client attached, Terminal firstWindowPreference (persistedWindowLayout restores a window already running zellij attach and then honours your command line on top of it, which is where the duplicate window came from), and it no longer counts any client as healthy. 0.7.1 - zac no longer opens a second window on a session something is already viewing. Zellij mirrors, so a second client is not a second session: it shows the same pane, takes your keystrokes in both, and sizes the grid to the SMALLEST client attached - which presents as a window that will not resize and text that will not reflow, and sends you looking at the terminal emulator rather than at the client list. It now tries the named window handle first and reads the Terminal window count to find out whether that focused a window or created one, because wt exits 0 either way. The Windows Terminal TAB is titled zellij-terminal rather than the session name; the session name was already on the zellij tab bar directly below it. zt check gained two lines: one when more than one client is attached, naming the resize symptom, and one for Terminal firstWindowPreference, because persistedWindowLayout restores a window that is already running zellij attach and then honours the command line on top of it - a cold attach opens two windows and nothing inside this rig can see why. 0.7.0 - the Command Palette extension had drifted eight verbs behind the module; it can now reach sync, publish, config (device and shared), validate, roots, define a root, export and import, plus previous tab. The rarer ones sit on a "registry and config" page rather than adding eight entries to a palette root shared with every other extension. Every palette action now logs its command and exit code to %LOCALAPPDATA%\ZellijTerminal\palette.log - the action path previously created no window and ended in an empty catch, so two verbs were completely dead for a day behind a toast that looked like success. Commands that block on Read-Host now get a console window automatically, decided in the constructor rather than per call site: "check the rig" had promised output "in a window" while running with CreateNoWindow, so it rendered where nobody could see and hung on its own prompt. 0.6.0 - the per-device registry moved OUT of the clone to %LOCALAPPDATA%\ZellijTerminal\devices\<HOST>.json. It is state this machine writes rather than source that ships, and in a working tree it died to git clean -xfd, to a re-clone, or to the directory being rebuilt underneath it. Nothing migrates automatically: copy your old config\devices\<HOST>.json to the new path, or set $env:ZT_CONFIG_HOME to the old directory to keep the previous layout - that variable relocates BOTH config files together and is how several PCs share one list through a private repo. config\workspaces.json is unchanged, because it is committed content that ships with the clone. zt check now prints which registry file it read, not just how many workspaces are in it. 0.5.0 - zt setup, a guided walkthrough that explains each layer before offering to do it and can install Zellij with winget; zt pad explain and zt palette, so the two optional pieces explain themselves rather than looking like broken required ones; the hook records its own failures to a log that zt check reports, closing the one place this rig could still fail silently; install.ps1 -Global registers the hook for every project rather than one repo, merged into existing settings; install.ps1 gates on PowerShell 7 and offers to install Zellij; zt add says which kind it registered and what will run, and -Kind pwsh no longer requires a command; Claude session display names drop the claude- tab prefix; a Pester suite and CI. 0.4.0 - zt pick and tab completion after zt; park/restore, so a shutdown is recovered from rather than caught; config/validate for hand-editing the JSON; an AGE column so a forgotten session is visible. 0.3.0 - renamed from ClaudeZellij to ZellijTerminal (zt). Adds a workspace registry split across a shared config in git and a per-device config only that device writes, live state under LOCALAPPDATA, start/stop/restart/close, tab-name collision detection, argument completion, and a zt dispatcher. 0.2.0 - Connect (zac). 0.1.0 - first cut wrapping the scripts.'
